<div class="page">

  <header>
    <div class="eyebrow">Integration plan &middot; 10 Sep 2026</div>
    <h1>AndeSim on FastSim</h1>
    <p class="lede">One SystemC platform, two CPU models. Today QEMU carries its own
      platform and vsim carries another one. Every device is written twice, and the
      two copies must agree.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>andesim v0.1.0</span>
      <span>fastsim v2 &middot; qbox &middot; qemu 10.2.3</span>
      <span>vsim_andesim</span>
      <span>hybrid_qemu</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="eyebrow">The goal</div>
      <h2>Keep one implementation of each device</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">The UART, the SimControl syscall window and the memory map exist
      twice: once in the QEMU fork, once in vsim. Each pair must stay in step by hand.
      The plan keeps one SystemC implementation of each, and lets both CPU models
      attach to it. FastSim already supplies that platform, so the work is on the
      vsim side, not on QBox.</p>

    <div class="out">
      <div>One FastSim v2 Lua platform holds both CPU models: <code>QemuInstance</code>
        (qbox) for the fast leg, a Verilated <code>VsimCore</code> for the
        cycle-accurate leg.</div>
      <div>One <code>SimControl</code>, one UART, one guest RAM. The QEMU-side mirror
        device is deleted, not ported.</div>
      <div>The ROI hand-off happens inside one process. No state file, no shared mmap
        file, no fd that stops working at the boundary.</div>
      <div>The cycle count does not change. A golden baseline proves it, fixture by
        fixture.</div>
      <div>The AndeSim driver gets thinner: the mmap tiers, the composed memory map
        and the two-process leg threading all go away. The memory map becomes one
        Lua file.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

