  <section>
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="eyebrow">Where we are today</div>
      <h2>Three repositories, three different distances</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="repos">

      <div class="repo">
        <div class="repo-id">
          <h3>FastSim</h3>
          <span class="chip chip-ok">ready</span>
          <span class="repo-path">/home/nick/work/fastsim</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li class="hit">QBox is already in place: <code>libs/qbox</code>, branch
            <code>fastsim-v2.0.0</code>, QEMU 10.2.3. Adopting QBox is not a decision
            we still have to make.</li>
          <li class="hit">The v2 platform is the target shape. In
            <code>conf/v2_platform.lua</code> the <code>router</code>,
            <code>gs_memory</code>, <code>Uart16550V2</code>, <code>StopperV2</code>
            and <code>plic_andes</code> are SystemC modules; QEMU is one
            <code>QemuInstance</code> module that only supplies a CPU.</li>
          <li><code>gs_memory</code> supports DMI through
            <code>get_direct_mem_ptr</code>
            <span class="ref">(docs/user/fastsim_sc_mem.md:10)</span>. This is what the
            vsim syscall host will resolve guest addresses through.</li>
          <li>Two designs already run in one process for mixed rv32/rv64 platforms, and
            the symbol trap that comes with it is known and fixed
            <span class="ref">(a9d893e)</span>.</li>
          <li>No Verilator model and no RTL exist in the tree. The cycle-accurate side
            is greenfield.</li>
          <li><code>conf/v2_ace_hybrid.lua</code> goes the other way: QEMU keeps
            <code>-M andes_ae350</code> and only one window is bridged out. That is the
            transitional shape, not the target.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="repo">
        <div class="repo-id">
          <h3>vsim</h3>
          <span class="chip chip-warn">closer than expected</span>
          <span class="repo-path">/home/nick/work/vsim_andesim</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li class="hit"><code>SimControl</code> is already a TLM-2.0 target module
            &mdash; <code>sc_core::sc_module</code> with a
            <code>simple_target_socket</code>
            <span class="ref">(src/platform/sim_control.hpp:51)</span>. It is already
            the shape FastSim's ModuleFactory wants, so stage 1 is a move, not a
            rewrite.</li>
          <li class="hit"><code>handle_open</code> returns the raw host fd, with no
            guest-fd table <span class="ref">(src/htif/sys_call.hpp)</span>. That is
            exactly why the fd does not survive the hand-off &mdash; and why one
            process removes the problem with no new code.</li>
          <li><code>guest_va_to_host()</code>
            <span class="ref">(src/htif/sys_call.hpp:35)</span> resolves into vsim's own
            memory buffer. Under FastSim it must resolve through
            <code>gs_memory</code> DMI. This is the one real piece of glue, and it sits
            behind a single function.</li>
          <li>The core is Verilated with SystemC output
            (<code>V&lt;engine&gt;/obj_sc</code>), so it can join an
            <code>sc_main</code> &mdash; but it is not yet separable from its own
            platform (<code>src/platform/</code>, <code>memory_map.h</code>).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="repo">
        <div class="repo-id">
          <h3>AndeSim</h3>
          <span class="chip chip-hold">works today</span>
          <span class="repo-path">/home/nick/work/andesim</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li>v0.1.0 is released and the hybrid measurement works. Nothing here is
            broken; the plan makes it smaller.</li>
          <li>The hand-off is one-way by design: QEMU drains to a
            <code>hybrid_state_v1</code> file and exits, then vsim resumes. Handback
            was removed on purpose <span class="ref">(driver/plan.hpp:8)</span>.</li>
          <li>Guest RAM crosses as a shared mmap file
            (<code>memory-backend-file,share=on</code>)
            <span class="ref">(driver/engine_registry.cpp:50)</span>.</li>
          <li>The state ABI carries no fd table at all
            <span class="ref">(include/hybrid/state_abi.h)</span>.</li>
          <li><code>tests/test_kernel_cycles.sh</code> checks that a cycle figure is
            printed. It does not pin the value, so a golden baseline does not exist
            yet. Stage 0 fixes that first.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="eyebrow">The work</div>
      <h2>Four stages, each one useful on its own</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">The stages are ordered by dependency, not by size. Stage 0 protects
      the number that the product sells. Stage 1 pays for itself before any RTL work
      starts.</p>

    <div class="stages">

      <div class="stage">
        <div class="stage-rail">
          <div class="stage-id">S0</div>
          <div class="stage-cost">small &middot; andesim only</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-body">
          <h3>Pin the number before anything moves</h3>
          <p>The cycle count is the product. Any change under the core can move it
            silently, so the guard must exist before the first line of integration
            code. Record <code>kernel_cycles</code> for a set of fixtures as a golden
            baseline, and write down the fidelity contract in the two halves that
            people confuse: the <em>bytes</em> of guest RAM may move out to the shared
            <code>gs_memory</code>, but the <em>timing</em> of every access stays
            inside the core.</p>
          <div class="gate">
            <b>Done when</b>
            The baseline test fails if a fixture's cycle count moves by one cycle.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="stage">
        <div class="stage-rail">
          <div class="stage-id">S1</div>
          <div class="stage-cost">small &middot; do this first</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-body">
          <h3>One syscall host</h3>
          <p>Register vsim's <code>SimControl</code> as a FastSim
            <code>moduletype</code>, bind its target socket to the <code>router</code>
            in Lua, and port <code>guest_va_to_host()</code> to
            <code>gs_memory</code> DMI. Then delete the mirror device in
            <code>hybrid_qemu</code>'s <code>andes_ae350.c</code>. No RTL work is
            needed for this stage.</p>
          <div class="gate">
            <b>Done when</b>
            AndeSim's file-I/O fixtures pass on the FastSim v2 platform with QEMU
            alone, and a file opened before the ROI still reads correctly after it.
          </div>
          <p class="drops"><b>Deletes:</b> the second implementation of 22 syscalls, and
            the fd problem, permanently.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="stage">
        <div class="stage-rail">
          <div class="stage-id">S2</div>
          <div class="stage-cost">large &middot; the main engineering</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-body">
          <h3>The RTL core becomes a platform citizen</h3>
          <p>Wrap the Verilated core as a SystemC module. Its bus pins keep the latency
            model they have today, so the cycle cost of a load still comes from the
            cache RTL; only the <em>bytes</em> change address, arriving through a DMI
            pointer into <code>gs_memory</code>. What we move is the storage, not the
            memory model.</p>
          <p>Device space is the one thing that becomes real TLM traffic on the router,
            and it costs nothing: vsim's <code>SimControl</code> already drops the TLM
            delay argument <span class="ref">(src/platform/sim_control.hpp:98)</span>,
            so it adds no time today either, and device space is PMA-uncached on every
            engine <span class="ref">(runtime/htif_client.h:5)</span>.</p>
          <div class="gate">
            <b>Done when</b>
            <code>run --mode cycle</code> runs on the FastSim platform and stage 0's
            golden numbers are unchanged.
          </div>
          <p class="drops"><b>Watch:</b> Verilator adds a third design to the process.
            Expect the <code>STB_GNU_UNIQUE</code> class of bug that a9d893e already
            fixed once.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="stage">
        <div class="stage-rail">
          <div class="stage-id">S3</div>
          <div class="stage-cost">medium &middot; the payoff</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stage-body">
          <h3>In-process hand-off</h3>
          <p>Replace the state file and the shared mmap with a direct architectural
            state copy between the two CPU modules. Both now address the same
            <code>gs_memory</code>, so guest RAM needs no transport at all.</p>
          <div class="gate">
            <b>Done when</b>
            <code>run --verify</code> still matches the QEMU oracle.
          </div>
          <p class="drops"><b>Deletes:</b> the mmap tier logic in
            <code>driver/shm.cpp</code>, the <code>hybrid_state_v1</code> file
            plumbing, the composed map in <code>driver/memmap.hpp</code>, and the
            two-process leg threading in <code>driver/run_plan.cpp</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="eyebrow">Known risks</div>
      <h2>What can go wrong</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="risks">
      <div class="risk">
        <h3>Cycle fidelity</h3>
        <p>The bytes may be shared; the latency may not. If a load took its timing from
          the loosely-timed <code>gs_memory</code> instead of from the cache RTL, the
          measured number would lose its meaning. Take the data through DMI, keep the
          latency model in the core. S0 is the guard, not a formality.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="risk">
        <h3>The vsim refactor is the bulk</h3>
        <p>The core must separate from its own platform. That work is in
          <code>vsim_andesim</code>, not in QBox, and it is where the schedule will be
          spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="risk">
        <h3>Symbols in one process</h3>
        <p>Vague-linkage statics bind across libraries even under
          <code>RTLD_DEEPBIND</code>. FastSim hit this once and has a tool for it;
          Verilator brings another design into the same process.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="risk">
        <h3>Fork sequencing</h3>
        <p>The platform patches in <code>hybrid_qemu</code> get deleted rather than
          ported. That is a gain, but the deletion must follow S1, not lead it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head">
      <div class="eyebrow">Open question</div>
      <h2>One decision to make first</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="decision">
      <h3>Must the accurate engine stay the RTL vsim?</h3>
      <p>FastSim already ships a per-instruction cycle model
        (<code>libacefsim_rv64.so</code>, <code>riscvhf::CycleModel</code>). If that
        accuracy is enough for some of our use cases, those cases work today and do not
        need S2 at all. If what we sell is RTL-derived confidence, S2 stands as
        written.</p>
      <p>The answer changes the size of this plan, not its shape: S0 and S1 are worth
        doing either way.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
